WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.548
Back to the legal assistant from the last lesson.

00:00:04.848 --> 00:00:09.573
One services contract, eighteen pages, about twelve thousand tokens.

00:00:10.223 --> 00:00:15.160
The pipeline splits it into five hundred token chunks, and retrieves the four

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.763
nearest to each question.

00:00:17.063 --> 00:00:20.084
Four chunks of five hundred is two thousand tokens.

00:00:20.384 --> 00:00:21.618
Out of twelve thousand.

00:00:22.268 --> 00:00:27.055
So when someone asked about terminating for late delivery, the model answered

00:00:27.055 --> 00:00:29.355
looking at one sixth of the document.

00:00:29.655 --> 00:00:30.776
And it didn't know that.

00:00:31.076 --> 00:00:32.500
It said no penalty.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.411
Clause fourteen point three said otherwise.

00:00:36.061 --> 00:00:40.845
In this lesson, you'll take the retrieval out, safely, put the whole contract in

00:00:40.845 --> 00:00:43.955
front of the model, and keep the bill under control.

00:00:45.155 --> 00:00:47.808
The refactoring fits in two sentences.

00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:52.978
Send the whole material in the context, in a fixed block at the start of the call.

00:00:53.278 --> 00:00:58.049
And turn on prompt caching, so you don't pay for the full read on every question.

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:03.705
Vector search exists to pick the little that fits, when the material is large.

00:01:04.005 --> 00:01:07.770
With small material, that choice only removes information.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.932
The split separates clauses that depend on each other, and the search misses when

00:01:12.932 --> 00:01:15.453
the question's words don't match the text.

00:01:16.653 --> 00:01:20.202
Before, the contract becomes chunks and embeddings.

00:01:20.502 --> 00:01:22.264
The question picks four of them.

00:01:22.564 --> 00:01:25.568
And the model answers with whatever the search chose.

00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:32.015
After, the whole document goes into a fixed block, right after the instructions.

00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:36.115
On the first question, that prefix is written to the cache.

00:01:36.765 --> 00:01:41.975
On the next ones, the model reads the contract from the cache, at a fraction of the

00:01:41.975 --> 00:01:42.289
cost.

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:47.653
And the answer can connect distant clauses, because nothing was cut.

00:01:48.853 --> 00:01:51.455
So let's ask the same question again.

00:01:51.755 --> 00:01:56.194
With four chunks, the context had termination, and not the penalty.

00:01:56.494 --> 00:02:00.342
With the whole contract, both clauses sit in the same prefix.

00:02:00.992 --> 00:02:04.852
And the instruction asks the model to cite article and paragraph.

00:02:05.152 --> 00:02:07.414
So the answer becomes this.

00:02:07.714 --> 00:02:08.233
No.

00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:13.304
Clause eight allows termination for delay, but fourteen point three applies a

00:02:13.304 --> 00:02:17.393
penalty on default, and late delivery falls under that definition.

00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:21.014
That's an answer a lawyer can check in ten seconds.

00:02:21.664 --> 00:02:23.291
One warning still stands.

00:02:23.591 --> 00:02:28.344
A huge context doesn't guarantee the model finds the clause in the middle.

00:02:28.644 --> 00:02:31.116
The research calls it lost in the middle.

00:02:31.416 --> 00:02:34.134
That's why the condition is fits with room to spare.

00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:37.910
Twelve thousand tokens in a large window is room.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:42.752
A hundred thousand tokens of stacked annexes is a different design.

00:02:43.952 --> 00:02:45.072
Here's one for you.

00:02:45.372 --> 00:02:50.879
After the switch, the user's second question shows zero tokens read from cache.

00:02:51.179 --> 00:02:55.866
Looking at the prompt, the system block starts with today's date, so the model knows

00:02:55.866 --> 00:02:57.596
the day, and then the contract.

00:02:57.896 --> 00:02:58.860
What's the fix?

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:07.022
Take the date out of the prefix, and send it in the user message, after the cached

00:03:07.022 --> 00:03:07.583
document.

00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:11.133
The cache reuses an identical start of the prompt.

00:03:11.433 --> 00:03:15.739
A date that changes every day means there's never anything to reuse.

00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:21.368
It's not the provider's fault, and caching the user message doesn't help either,

00:03:21.368 --> 00:03:23.795
because that changes on every question.

00:03:24.095 --> 00:03:26.602
Instructions and contract stay stable.

00:03:26.902 --> 00:03:29.566
Date, user and question come after.

00:03:30.766 --> 00:03:32.051
Now the mechanics.

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:34.667
Step zero, measure.

00:03:34.967 --> 00:03:40.924
Run the question eval, and include questions that span sections, like a rule and its

00:03:40.924 --> 00:03:41.633
exception.

00:03:41.933 --> 00:03:45.888
Record accuracy, cost and latency per question.

00:03:46.188 --> 00:03:49.559
Those cross-section questions are the reason for the swap.

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:51.189
Check the size.

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:56.135
Count the tokens with the provider's token counter, not a guess.

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:59.241
Leave room for the conversation and the answer.

00:03:59.541 --> 00:04:02.733
If there's no room, this refactoring doesn't apply.

00:04:03.383 --> 00:04:05.050
Build the fixed prefix.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.976
Instructions first, then the document.

00:04:08.276 --> 00:04:11.407
And nothing that varies before the end of the document.

00:04:11.707 --> 00:04:14.857
No date, no user id, no time.

00:04:15.507 --> 00:04:18.101
Turn on prompt caching on the document block.

00:04:18.751 --> 00:04:20.010
Confirm it works.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:24.932
From the second call on, the usage report should show tokens read from cache.

00:04:25.232 --> 00:04:28.566
If it shows zero, the prefix is changing somewhere.

00:04:29.216 --> 00:04:33.694
Measure again, and compare accuracy and cost per question.

00:04:33.994 --> 00:04:37.469
The cross-section questions are the test that matters.

00:04:37.769 --> 00:04:42.657
In the example, retrieval scored sixty one percent, and the whole document scored

00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:43.442
eighty eight.

00:04:44.092 --> 00:04:48.758
And only after the new version is stable, remove the retrieval pipeline.

00:04:49.058 --> 00:04:51.648
Don't leave it behind a flag, just in case.

00:04:51.948 --> 00:04:57.050
While both exist, someone will just tune the number of chunks, during an incident.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.293
A few risks.

00:04:59.593 --> 00:05:00.932
The material grows.

00:05:01.232 --> 00:05:02.811
Today it's one contract.

00:05:03.111 --> 00:05:08.571
Tomorrow it's a hundred and twenty vendor contracts, and the prefix no longer fits

00:05:08.571 --> 00:05:09.237
with room.

00:05:09.537 --> 00:05:11.880
Size changed, so the tool changes.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.891
Go back to an index, filtered by the user's contract, and keep direct context for

00:05:16.891 --> 00:05:18.985
the single document that still fits.

00:05:19.635 --> 00:05:21.209
Widely spaced questions.

00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:24.906
The cache expires after a few minutes without use.

00:05:25.206 --> 00:05:28.108
With low traffic, the discount shows up less.

00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:32.177
The accuracy gain on cross-clause questions still holds.

00:05:32.827 --> 00:05:34.642
Parts the user may not see.

00:05:34.942 --> 00:05:40.083
If some users may only see the pricing annex, a prompt asking the model to hide the

00:05:40.083 --> 00:05:41.756
rest is not access control.

00:05:42.056 --> 00:05:43.632
A leading question leaks it.

00:05:43.932 --> 00:05:49.514
Permission applies before the context is built, with filtered retrieval, or a prefix

00:05:49.514 --> 00:05:50.112
per role.

00:05:50.762 --> 00:05:53.212
And thinking that it fits is enough.

00:05:53.512 --> 00:05:59.007
If the cross-section eval stays poor with the whole document, the problem wasn't

00:05:59.007 --> 00:05:59.694
retrieval.

00:05:59.994 --> 00:06:05.103
It may be the prompt, the model, or a contract that's genuinely ambiguous.

00:06:05.753 --> 00:06:06.870
And a quick boundary.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:12.832
An online store thinks about pasting its catalog of eight thousand products into the

00:06:12.832 --> 00:06:14.247
prefix, with caching.

00:06:14.547 --> 00:06:16.124
That's not this refactoring.

00:06:16.424 --> 00:06:21.491
It doesn't fit with room, each question needs a few rows, and a product's price is a

00:06:21.491 --> 00:06:22.637
lookup by its code.

00:06:22.937 --> 00:06:27.382
That's a query tool, or retrieval, not an eight thousand item prefix.

00:06:28.582 --> 00:06:31.038
The code gets shorter, which is a good sign.

00:06:31.688 --> 00:06:34.302
The contract is loaded once, as text.

00:06:34.952 --> 00:06:37.005
The system part has two blocks.

00:06:37.305 --> 00:06:41.078
The instructions, and then the contract, marked for caching.

00:06:41.728 --> 00:06:46.545
And the question goes alone in the user message, after everything that's stable.

00:06:46.845 --> 00:06:51.712
The three lines that used to embed the question, search the database and join the

00:06:51.712 --> 00:06:53.095
chunks are simply gone.

00:06:53.745 --> 00:06:57.510
On the page, you'll find the same change across the code tabs.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:03.189
What changes between them is only how each one marks the block for caching, and

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:06.186
where it reports the tokens read from cache.

00:07:07.386 --> 00:07:09.573
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:09.873 --> 00:07:15.093
When the material fits with room to spare, retrieval is a filter that only removes.

00:07:15.393 --> 00:07:19.787
Put the document first, the question after, let the cache carry the cost, and

00:07:19.787 --> 00:07:22.354
measure on the questions that cross sections.

00:07:23.004 --> 00:07:28.011
But sometimes the material really doesn't fit, and you do need chunks.

00:07:28.311 --> 00:07:33.534
In the next lesson, we'll make each chunk carry the context it lost when it was cut:

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:34.777
contextual chunking.

00:07:35.077 --> 00:07:36.184
See you there.
